    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d86">

                <pb facs="0177" n="143" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v15p1/pg143"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">795.00/4–352: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Commander in Chief United Nations Command
                            (Ridgway) to the
                        Assistant Chief of Staff G–3, Department of
                        the Army (Jenkins)</hi><anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d86-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top
                            secret<lb/>operational immediate</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1952-04-12T17:29:00+09:00">April 12, 1952—5:29
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>C 66832. Re opn <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Scatter</hi>.</p>
                <p>1. The cumulative results of the screening operation as of 2400, 11 Apr 52 are:
                    Read in 3 cols, col 1 total screened, col 2 total to return to Communist
                    control, col 3 total indicating they would forcibly resist repatriation. <pb
                        facs="0178" n="144" xml:id="pg144"/>
                    <table cols="4" rows="5">
                        <row>
                            <cell/>
                            <cell role="num">1</cell>
                            <cell role="num">2</cell>
                            <cell role="num">3</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>North Korean POWs</cell>
                            <cell role="num">51491</cell>
                            <cell role="num">21102</cell>
                            <cell role="num">30389</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>Chinese POWs</cell>
                            <cell role="num">17593</cell>
                            <cell role="num">3467</cell>
                            <cell role="num">14126</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>South Korean POWs</cell>
                            <cell role="num">14616</cell>
                            <cell role="num">3559</cell>
                            <cell role="num">11057</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>Civilian Internees</cell>
                            <cell role="num">21303</cell>
                            <cell role="num">2628</cell>
                            <cell role="num">18675</cell>
                        </row>
                    </table></p>
                <p>2. As a result of the screening completed in opn <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">Scatter</hi> to date approximately
                    44,000 North Koreans, South Koreans and CIs have
                    either refused screening or are in compounds where prisoners representatives
                    object to their compounds being subjected to screening. In addition, some 12,000
                        POWs and CIs in the hospital area in camp nr 10, Pusan, have not been
                    screened.</p>
                <p>3. To determine a reliable round number and accurate rosters for submission to
                    the Communists, a plan for screening or otherwise designating those POWs and CIs
                    mentioned in para 2 above is being developed.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
